   "en": {
    "headline": "\"AI promised less work and I've never worked more\": a blog post sparks a debate on the workload of agents",
    "summary_short": "A tech executive uses their own GitHub data to show that, with AI agents, they work more than ever. The widely discussed post opens a debate on the fatigue of managing many agents at once.",
    "summary_long": "The author, Vice President of Technology at Bold.org, uses agents for all of their engineering work. The thesis: AI has not given them time back, but more work. They used to handle two or three serious tasks a week; now several agents run in parallel and each one occasionally needs them to review, approve or decide. That constant context switching is more draining than the work they did before.\n\nTheir GitHub data reflects it: 2,102 contributions by October 27, more than in any year from 2021 to 2025, and a monthly record in August, with 427. The author describes cycles: a new model or workflow brings a flood of ideas, coding is cheap so they chase all of them and, weeks later, they end up tired of AI. The conclusion: being able to do more turns into doing more. The post drew more than 600 comments on Hacker News.",
    "unconfirmed_claims": []
